<mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 1px 1px 1px" border-radius="10px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="10px" padding="0px">
    <mj-image
      border-radius="10px 10px 0 0"
      padding="0"
      width="600px"
      src="https://www.londonsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
      alt="North London outbreak"
      href="https://www.londonsummary.com/"
    />
    <mj-text
      padding="20px 20px 0px 20px"
      font-family="Austin News Text Web, TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif"
      color="#000000"
    >
      <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin: 0;">
        North London outbreak
      </h2>
    </mj-text>
    <mj-text padding="0px 20px 0px 20px" font-family="Arial" color="#000000">
      <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; padding-top: 15px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>What’s happening?</strong> Measles cases are rising in north London, with the boroughs of Enfield and Haringey accounting for nearly half of all reported infections in England. Enfield recorded 12 new cases in the final week of February, bringing its total to 71 this year, while Haringey has reported 13 cases over the past two months. London currently accounts for about <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cwy9xd67z85o" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #80011F; color: black;">65% of England’s measles cases</a>.
</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; padding-top: 15px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>Why?</strong> In some areas of London, uptake of the measles, mumps, and rubella (MMR) vaccine has fallen below the level required to prevent outbreaks. The outbreak is mainly affecting young children in schools and nurseries, with the most severe cases linked to unvaccinated patients. Delayed vaccinations following the COVID-19 pandemic and lingering vaccine hesitancy among some parents have also contributed.
</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 24px; padding-top: 15px; padding-bottom: 20px; margin: 0;">
  <strong>Big picture:</strong> Measles had been largely suppressed through widespread immunisation, but gaps in vaccine coverage have created conditions for new outbreaks. Health authorities warn that London’s surge could spread to other regions if vaccination rates are not quickly improved. The situation also reflects wider global trends, as measles cases have risen in several countries following pandemic-related disruptions.
</p>
    </mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="20px" />
    
